esterns are gritty and grounded by  action scenes populated by lifelike  characters but taking this more stylized  route and giving hex superpowers  challenges the genre in the wrong kind  of way and what does this whole new  direction accomplish pretty much nothing  with the film only making sporadic use  of hex's newfound abilities  every major superhero movie inevitably  ends with a big confrontation between  the hero and villain in the case of  2004's catwoman this is the battle  between the titular superhero and evil  cosmetics executive laurel herder the  problem with the sequence isn't that  it's offensive or thematically  inconsistent but simply that it tries  way too hard all throughout catwoman  seems like the infamous flirty  basketball game make it clear this is a  production that would love to be seen as  a campy good time the finale with very  grimly and toning lines like this is no  different  it's  overtime  unfortunately catwoman lacks the  creative unpredictability that defines  great pieces of camp arts like say the  rocky horror picture show or the works  of john waters those projects work  because they follow a unique creative  vision not a rigid blueprint for what  campy cinema should look like the latter  approach plays catwoman's final showdown  despite an abundance of cheesy lines and  the participation of camp queen sharon  stone the film's finale is dominated by  predictable action beats leaning so hard  into those elements delivers a climax  that's uncomfortably lackluster rather  than deliriously campy  as the original suicide squad film comes  to a close harley quinn is returned to  her prison cell only for the joker and  his goons to suddenly show up and break  her out of prison as alarm bells quite  literally ring joker embraces quinn and  remarks to her that it's time for them  to go home this romantic reunion is an  odd way to end an ensemble effort like  suicide squad since it narrows the focus  exclusively to quinn but it also goes  too far in sugarcoating the duo's toxic  relationship alterations to suicide  squad drawing reshoots and  post-production ended up cutting a  subplot involving quinn abandoning and  even beating up joker in favour of her  new comrades this development was  eschewed in favour of an ending that  leaves no room for moral ambiguity nor  any recognition of just how abusive the  joker's behavior has been it's tidy sure  but it fits neither the characters nor  their relationship and closes the movie  out on a note that leaves a bad taste in  the mouth  it's been discussed at nauseam by  everyone from comics writers to fans to  the cast and crew of the movie itself  even with this avalanche of discourse  however the snapping of zod's neck at  the end of man of steel still stands out  as a premier example of a dc comics  movie going too far at the end of the  film that launched the ill-fated snyder  verse superman is forced to choose  between killing zod or allowing him to  kill a group of innocent civilians the  hero opts for the former snapping zod's  neck and then proceeding to wail in  misery over killing the only other  surviving member of his species  [Applause]  the concept behind this moment isn't  inherently bad especially for a superman  origin story in execution though it  doesn't have much impact coming off a  lengthy sequence of endless fist  fighting between the two kryptonians how  can a neck snap compare to all of this  what's more neither the rest of man of  steel nor subsequent dc films pause for  any length of time to absorb the full  consequences of superman's actions at  this point you can't help but get the  feeling that superman was only made to  do this in order to stoke some fan  controversy  in alan moore and dave gibbons original  watchmen graphic novel the character of  rorschach makes a comment in his journal  suggesting that fellow hero ozymandias  might be gay this comment along with  other small details from the graphic  novel has led to wi